# Family A12: the Redis client library (benchmarks/redisclient).
# See hack/bakeoff.sh.
#
# A12's two arms are two client LIBRARIES -- rueidis and go-redis v9 -- so the
# package that constructs both has to import both, and no package in the root
# module can: the root module is standard-library-only (`make stdlib-check`,
# depguard's stdlib-only rule). Until now `hack/bakeoff.sh` ran one
# `GOWORK=off go test "$PKG"` from the repository root, so a family outside the
# root module could not be measured at all -- the constraint `hack/FOLLOWUPS.md`
# records from B04, and the reason family A15's kinds live in `driverkit/nack`
# rather than in `drivers/kafka`. A15 could escape it because its kinds are
# broker-independent TYPES; A11's and A12's cannot, because their kinds are the
# third-party libraries themselves.
#
# MODDIR names the module PKG is relative to, and `hack/bakeoff.sh` moves all
# four of its `go test` invocations there with `go test -C` -- the correctness
# gate, the kind list, the filter probe and the measurement, because a gate run
# in one module and a measurement in another describe different code.
MODDIR=benchmarks
PKG=./redisclient
BENCH=BenchmarkRedisClient
REF=rueidis
AUTO_KEY=redisclient
KIND_DIM=""
# ADR-013 collects the §9 rows that need a broker and are therefore decided in
# Part B (the plan's "Phase-0 rows that need a broker (A11-A13, A15-A17,
# A32-A35) are decided in Part B (ADR-013)").
ADR=13
TITLE="Redis client library: rueidis against go-redis v9 (A12)"
#
# Why the `benchmarks` module rather than a new one: it is the benchmarks
# module, it already depends on iqmq, and a new module would add a `go.work`
# entry, a `replace` block in every dependant and a second place to keep the
# client pins in step. It is deliberately NOT `benchmarks/native-baselines`,
# which was the first guess: that module measures RAW clients and its own
# `TestNoRootImport` refuses any import of `github.com/iqhive/iqmq`, because a
# native baseline that reached into iqmq would stop being a baseline. This
# family's arms are the drivers' own adapters, so they import iqmq by
# definition. The guard caught it, which is what it is for.
#
# What is held constant, and why it is an in-process server.
#
# The seam is `internal/streams.Commander`, which is the whole of what either
# driver needs from a client library, and everything above it -- the key
# naming, the field layout, the commands, the reclaim accounting, the nack
# strategies -- is code the two drivers SHARE. Measuring the driver would
# therefore measure that shared code twice and add its noise to the quantity
# being compared, so the family measures the adapter and the library: the same
# `internal/streams` command values, the same server, the same commands in the
# same order.
#
# The server is `internal/streams/fakestreams` behind
# `internal/streams/respsrv`, in process, over loopback TCP. A container would
# put a real server's own work -- its radix tree, its append-only file, its
# scheduler -- in front of every measurement, and that work is IDENTICAL for
# the two arms, so it is pure noise added to exactly what §9.1's criterion
# reads. What the in-process server cannot measure is a real network's
# latency, and docs/drivers/goredis.md says so.

#
# CRITERION is the benchmark configuration's decision column for this row, quoted.
# MATRIX is the IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX grid the campaign measures the family at.
# DECIDED_AT names the benchmark-name segments the criterion is read at, and
# DECIDED_ON the metrics; `make bench-lint` lists the family's benchmarks at
# the declared grid and fails when a term or a metric is missing from the rows
# every kind has in common, which is the only data `bakeoff decide` reads.
CRITERION="throughput/connection & allocs -> rueidis expected"
# MATRIX=default is three pipelining depths, two fetch counts and the 10,000
# pending-entries reclaim; `full` adds depth=256, count=128 and a small-PEL
# reclaim. The default grid already covers every term below, which is what a
# criterion read on throughput per connection and allocations needs -- the
# connection model is what differs, and it differs at every depth.
MATRIX=default
DECIDED_AT="publish pipeline consume reclaim block depth=1 depth=8 depth=64 count=1 count=16 pel=10000"
DECIDED_ON="ns/op allocs/op B/op"
# §9.1 row A12 also names "BLOCK latency" and "reclaim under 10k PEL", and both
# ARE benchmark rows here (`block/depth=1` and `reclaim/pel=10000`). What is not
# a benchmark row is a real network's latency: every figure is a loopback round
# trip to an in-process server, so the absolute numbers are far below what a
# deployment sees and the family is decided on the RATIO between two arms
# measured the same way. The correctness half of the row -- that the two
# libraries read one server identically -- is TestModel and FuzzRedisClientOps
# in the correctness gate, not a metric.
CRITERION_GAP="network latency=an in-process server measures the client and not the link -- the absolute ns/op is a loopback round trip and only the ratio between arms is read"
# The family's decision rule is `rules["A12"]` in
# benchmarks/cmd/bakeoff/rules.go. Both arms SHIP -- §7.2 asks for two Redis
# drivers -- so the "winner" is which library the driver page recommends and
# which one the broker-version matrix is run against, not which one is deleted;
# `AutoKind` in redisclient/auto.go records it and binds nothing at run time,
# because a caller chooses a client library by which module it imports.
